00:03:38,571 --> 00:03:44,571
It ain't over 'til it's over.

2
00:07:17,142 --> 00:07:23,142
Rocky Balboa is a drama film directed by Sylvester Stallone.

3
00:10:55,714 --> 00:11:01,714
When he loses a highly publicized virtual boxing match to ex-champ Rocky Balboa, reigning heavyweight
titleholder Mason Dixon retaliates by challenging the Italian Stallion to a 10-round exhibition bout.

4
00:14:34,285 --> 00:14:40,285
To the surprise of his son and friends, Rocky agrees to come out of retirement and
face an opponent who's faster, stronger and thirty years his junior.

5
00:18:12,857 --> 00:18:18,857
Rocky takes on Dixon in what will become the greatest fight in boxing history!

6
00:21:51,428 --> 00:21:57,428
Robert 'Rocky' Balboa, played by Sylvester Stallone.

7
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:36,000
Paulie Pennino, played by Burt Young.

8
00:29:08,571 --> 00:29:14,571
Mason 'The Line' Dixon, played by Antonio Tarver.

9
00:32:47,142 --> 00:32:53,142
Marie, played by Geraldine Hughes.

10
00:36:25,714 --> 00:36:31,714
Robert 'Rocky' Balboa Jr., played by Milo Ventimiglia.

11
00:40:04,285 --> 00:40:10,285
Tony 'Duke' Evers, played by Tony Burton.

12
00:43:42,857 --> 00:43:48,857
Rocky Balboa is a 2006 American sports drama film starring,
written and directed by Sylvester Stallone.

13
00:47:21,428 --> 00:47:27,428
It is the sequel to Rocky V (1990) and the sixth
installment in the Rocky franchise.

14
00:51:00,000 --> 00:51:06,000
The film co-stars Burt Young and Antonio Tarver in his only acting role.

15
00:54:38,571 --> 00:54:44,571
In the film, Rocky Balboa (Stallone), now an aging small restaurant owner, is
challenged to an exhibition fight by hothead young boxer Mason Dixon (Tarver).

16
00:58:17,142 --> 00:58:23,142
Development for a sixth Rocky film began after Stallone expressed regret over the outcome
of Rocky V, which was viewed as a disappointing conclusion to the series.

17
01:01:55,714 --> 01:02:01,714
Rocky Balboa includes references to characters and objects from previous installments, and
Stallone was inspired by recent personal struggles and triumphs when writing the film.

18
01:05:34,285 --> 01:05:40,285
It is Stallone's first directorial effort since Rocky IV (1985).

19
01:09:12,857 --> 01:09:18,857
Principal photography began in December 2005 and lasted until January 2006, with
filming locations including Las Vegas, Los Angeles, and Philadelphia.

20
01:12:51,428 --> 01:12:57,428
In contrast to previous entries in the franchise, the fight choreography in Rocky Balboa
was less scripted, featuring real punches thrown by Stallone and Tarver.

21
01:16:30,000 --> 01:16:36,000
It was produced by Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer, Columbia Pictures, Revolution Studios, Chartoff
Productions and Winkler Films, and theatrically released by MGM Distribution Co.

22
01:20:08,571 --> 01:20:14,571
in North America and 20th Century Fox internationally on December 20, 2006, sixteen years after
the release of Rocky V and thirty years after the release of the first film.

23
01:23:47,142 --> 01:23:53,142
It received generally positive reviews from critics, with praise for its screenplay, Stallone's performance, and heartfelt exploration of Balboa's character,
with many critics calling it a significant improvement over its predecessor, and many labelling the film one of the best entries in the franchise.

24
01:27:25,714 --> 01:27:31,714
It was also a commercial success, grossing $156 million worldwide.

25
01:31:04,285 --> 01:31:10,285
A spin-off, Creed, was released in 2015 and started its own series.

26
01:34:42,857 --> 01:34:48,857
The film explores themes of philadelphia, pennsylvania,
transporter, love of one's life, fight, tombstone.

27
01:38:21,428 --> 01:38:27,428
The tone is uplifting and emotional.
